  "summary": "A Santa Clara County Superior Court judge dismissed an ACLU/EFF lawsuit challenging San Jose's warrantless police access to its 500+ Flock ALPR cameras, finding no reasonable expectation of privacy on public streets. Plaintiffs are weighing an appeal, and a separate federal case continues. The ruling comes amid local controversy over an officer fired for misusing ALPR data and council debates over tighter safeguards.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Santa Clara County Superior Court Judge Jeffrey El-Hajj rejected arguments that the city's practice of allowing warrantless police searches of its automated license plate reader (ALPR) data \u2014 derived from a network of more than 500 devices scattered across town \u2014 violates the California Constitution's guarantee of privacy rights and prohibition on unreasonable searches.",
      "paragraph_idx": 2
    },
    {
      "quote": "We believe strongly that the ruling is wrong on the law, and all options for next steps are on the table",
      "paragraph_idx": 8
    },
    {
      "quote": "the San Jose Police Department fired an officer in April for allegedly sharing location data with his cousin",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    }
  ],
